Peritonsillar Abscess
Opthalmology & ENT
Unilateral sore throat, Uvular deviation, Trismus/'hot potato' voice

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-034
Reason for Visit: Extreme difficulty swallowing, inability to fully open the mouth, and severe unilateral throat pain following a presumed tonsillitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapidly progressing, severe, disproportionately unilateral sore throat accompanied by trismus, a muffled voice, and drooling. This classic presentation demands an urgent face-to-face assessment to diagnose a peritonsillar abscess (Quinsy) and evaluate for immediate airway compromise, dehydration, and the need for emergent surgical drainage prior to catastrophic spontaneous rupture or airway occlusion during the night.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't even swallow my own spit, my jaw is locked tight, and my throat is throbbing on the right side."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began 4 days ago as a generalized sore throat, presumed to be viral. Over the past 24 hours, the pain became severe, disproportionately unilateral, and associated with profound odynophagia. Patient has developed a muffled, thick, 'hot potato' voice quality. Endorses significant trismus, finding it severely difficult and painful to voluntarily open the mouth. Reports pooling of saliva and drooling due to an absolute inability to comfortably swallow. Experiences high-grade fever, systemic malaise, and rigors.
Functional Status: Weakened, continuously spitting saliva into a cup, visibly toxic.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of airway compromise and severe dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 118 | Temp: 39.2 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, acutely distressed, holding a cup to catch drool, speaking with a classic thick, muffled voice.
Physical Exam: Pronounced trismus limits mouth opening to approximately 20mm. Intraoral examination (with difficult visualization) reveals markedly asymmetric, tense, erythematous swelling of the right soft palate and peritonsillar tissues. The right tonsil is displaced medially and anteriorly. The uvula is distinctly deviated away from the affected side (to the left). Tender, swollen right-sided cervical and submandibular lymphadenopathy is palpable. Halitosis is prominent. No stridor, tachypnea, or tripod positioning.
Environmental Observations: Tissues and a spit cup utilized continuously.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Has been attempting to take oral penicillin prescribed yesterday but cannot physically swallow the tablets.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Right Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Status: Acute, localized emergency
Differentials:

Uncomplicated Streptococcal Tonsillitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound asymmetry, uvular deviation, localized soft palate fluctuance, and the presence of severe trismus (which points to involvement of the pterygoid musculature outside the tonsillar capsule).

Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct, visualized focal swelling in the peritonsillar space and uvular deviation, rather than a generalized supraglottic inflammation, though both share drooling and severe odynophagia.

Parapharyngeal Space Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The swelling being clearly localized to the superior peritonsillar pole displacing the tonsil inferomedially, rather than medial displacement of the entire lateral pharyngeal wall without tonsillar involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Airway risk, alone)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook peritonsillar abscess, exhibiting the classic triad of trismus, uvular deviation, and a 'hot potato' voice. Urgent bedside evaluation was critical to directly inspect the airway, confirming that while the oropharynx was severely narrowed, there was no immediate glottic obstruction (stridor). Because the patient had progressed to absolute inability to swallow oral secretions or previously prescribed oral antibiotics, community management failed. Immediate escalation to a tertiary center was mandated for intravenous hydration, IV antibiotics, and emergent needle aspiration/incision and drainage to secure the airway and definitively resolve the abscess.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered zero further oral medications due to severe aspiration risk and absolute dysphagia. Arranged immediate Code 2 Ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary Emergency Department for ENT consultation, intravenous therapy, and procedural drainage.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately minimize the risk of sudden abscess rupture and subsequent aspiration of large volumes of purulent material while awaiting transport, the patient must strictly maintain an upright, forward-leaning posture and utilize the spit cup exclusively, strictly refusing to attempt to swallow. The physical act of expectorating relieves the agonizing stretching of the pharyngeal muscles (positive reinforcement), ensuring airway safety until surgical intervention occurs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handed over to tertiary emergency/ENT service.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being actively transported via ambulance. Explicit handover provided to paramedics regarding the severe trismus and potential for sudden, difficult airway management if spontaneous rupture occurs during transit.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a severe, deep tissue infection requiring hospital intervention.
Clinical Course: The abscess requires physical drainage by an ENT specialist. Once drained, the severe pain and inability to swallow resolve almost instantaneously.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat, drink, or swallow your own saliva. Keep leaning forward. The ambulance is on its way.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A severe infection has formed a large pocket of pus behind your tonsil, pushing it across your throat and locking your jaw muscles. Because you cannot swallow tablets safely, you must go to the hospital to have the pus drained and receive antibiotics straight into your vein.
Actionable Steps: Sit upright and lean slightly forward. Spit all saliva into the cup provided. Do not forcefully clear your throat or cough hard, as this could prematurely burst the abscess.

